# Finding things

Two tools: fzf-lua, a fuzzy finder for files, text, buffers and more, and
nvim-tree, a NerdTree-style file tree. Start with a fresh playground:

    :Playground!

Everything searches the playground's working directory. Come back here with
`gT`{normal}.

# Lesson 2.1: Finding files

`<Space><Space>`{normal} (or `<Space>ff`{normal}) lists every file in the project. Type a few
letters from anywhere in the path; they don't need to be next to each other.
`oct ide` finds `notes/2026/october/ideas.md`.

Inside any fzf-lua picker:

    `<C-n>`{normal} / `<C-p>`{normal}   move down / up (arrow keys also work)
    `<Enter>`{normal}         open
    `<C-v>`{normal}           open in a vertical split
    `<C-s>`{normal}           open in a horizontal split
    `<C-t>`{normal}           open in a new tab
    `<Tab>`{normal}           mark several entries; `<Enter>`{normal} then opens all of them
    `<S-Down>`{normal} / `<S-Up>`{normal}  scroll the preview
    `<F4>`{normal}            hide / show the preview
    `<F1>`{normal}            every key available in this picker
    `<Esc>`{normal}           close

 1. Press `<Space><Space>`{normal}, find the October ideas file and open it.
    What is the codename of the next release?

    codename: ____

 2. Press `<Space><Space>`{normal} again, find `inventory.py` and open it in a vertical
    split with `<C-v>`{normal}.

# Lesson 2.2: Searching text

    `<Space>fg`{normal}   live grep: results update as you type (ripgrep regex)
    `<Space>fw`{normal}   grep the word under the cursor (in visual mode: the selection)
    `<Space>f/`{normal}   fuzzy-search the lines of the current buffer
    `<Space>fr`{normal}   reopen the last picker, with its query and position

In live grep, `<C-g>`{normal} switches to fuzzy-filtering the current results, which
is handy for narrowing a big result list by file name.

 1. Press `<Space>fg`{normal} and type `treasure`. Which value is stored next to it?

    treasure: ____

 2. Press `<Esc>`{normal}, then `<Space>fr`{normal}. Your search is back where you left it.

# Lesson 2.3: Buffers, history and help

    `<Space>fb`{normal}   open buffers
    `<Space>fo`{normal}   recently opened files, across sessions
    `<Space>fh`{normal}   Neovim help tags
    `<Space>fk`{normal}   every keymap, searchable by description

 1. Press `<Space>fk`{normal} and type `toggle terminal` to see which key does it.
 2. Press `<Space>fo`{normal}. The playground files you opened in the last lesson
    are at the top.

# Lesson 2.4: The file tree

    `<Space>n`{normal}    toggle the tree
    `<Space>N`{normal}    open the tree with the current file revealed

Inside the tree:

    `<Enter>`{normal} or `o`{normal}   open file / expand or collapse folder
    `<C-v>`{normal} `<C-x>`{normal} `<C-t>`{normal}  open in vertical split / split / tab
    `<Tab>`{normal}          preview the file, keeping focus in the tree
    `P`{normal}              go to the parent folder
    `a`{normal}              create a file; end the name with `/` for a folder
    `r`{normal}              rename (`e`{normal}: rename only the base name)
    `d`{normal}              delete
    `x`{normal} `c`{normal} `p`{normal}          cut / copy / paste
    `y`{normal} `Y`{normal} `gy`{normal}         copy the name / relative path / absolute path
    `f`{normal}              filter the tree as you type (`F`{normal} clears it)
    `H`{normal}              show / hide dotfiles
    `E`{normal} `W`{normal}            expand / collapse everything
    `q`{normal}              close the tree
    `g?`{normal}             all tree keys

 1. Open `notes/2026/october/ideas.md` (with `<Space><Space>`{normal}) and press `<Space>N`{normal}. The
    tree opens on that file. Which other folder sits next to `october`?

    sibling folder: ____

 2. In the tree, put the cursor on the `notes` folder, press `a`{normal} and create
    `todo.md`. Then rename it with `r`{normal} to `todo-later.md`, and delete it with `d`{normal}.

# Summary

    `<Space><Space>`{normal} files   `<Space>fg`{normal} grep   `<Space>fw`{normal} word   `<Space>f/`{normal} buffer lines
    `<Space>fb`{normal} buffers      `<Space>fo`{normal} recent `<Space>fr`{normal} resume `<Space>fk`{normal} keymaps
    `<Space>n`{normal} tree          `<Space>N`{normal} reveal file in tree

